Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns, what they will be used for, and special conditions  
• accident_2016.state_number – numeric predictor (will also be used to join).  
• vehicle_2016.body_type – numeric predictor (one record / vehicle; will be aggregated to the crash).  
• accident_2016.number_of_drunk_drivers – numeric predictor.  
• accident_2016.day_of_week – numeric predictor.  
• accident_2016.hour_of_crash – numeric predictor.  
• accident_2016.work_zone – build binary indicator work_zone_flag (0 = “None”, 1 = all other codes).  
• vehicle_2016.travel_speed – needed for engineered feature; use only values 0‑151 (exclude 997, 998, 999).  
• vehicle_2016.speed_limit – needed for engineered feature; use only values 5‑80 (exclude 0, 98, 99).  
• person_2016.injury_severity – create the target (injury_severity = 4 → fatal).  
• person_2016.person_number – to count distinct persons in an accident.  
• state_number + consecutive_number – join keys across all three tables.  

Conditions / filters that will be applied  
• Keep only accidents that involve > 1 distinct person.  
• travel_speed BETWEEN 0 AND 151      -- drop 997/998/999  
• speed_limit BETWEEN 5 AND 80       -- drop 0/98/99  
• Engineer avg_abs_speed_diff = AVG(ABS(travel_speed‑speed_limit)) per accident.  
 Then bucket:  
 0 if [0,20); 1 if [20,40); 2 if [40,60); 3 if [60,80); 4 if ≥ 80.  
• Label = 1 if #fatalities ( injury_severity = 4 ) > 1, otherwise 0.  

Below are ten exploratory‑to‑final queries (LIMIT 20 each) that progressively build toward the labeled data set.

```sql
-- 1) Description: peek at core accident columns (time and work‑zone flag)
SELECT
  state_number,
  consecutive_number,
  day_of_week,
  hour_of_crash,
  work_zone,
  number_of_drunk_drivers
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2016`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Description: look at vehicle body types and speed related columns
SELECT
  state_number,
  consecutive_number,
  vehicle_number,
  body_type,
  travel_speed,
  speed_limit
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.vehicle_2016`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Description: inspect person table for injury severity values
SELECT
  state_number,
  consecutive_number,
  vehicle_number,
  person_number,
  injury_severity
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.person_2016`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Description: count how many distinct persons are involved in each accident
SELECT
  state_number,
  consecutive_number,
  COUNT(DISTINCT person_number) AS persons_in_crash
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.person_2016`
GROUP BY state_number, consecutive_number
HAVING persons_in_crash > 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Description: count number of fatalities (injury_severity = 4) per accident
SELECT
  state_number,
  consecutive_number,
  COUNTIF(injury_severity = 4) AS fatalities_in_crash
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.person_2016`
GROUP BY state_number, consecutive_number
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Description: compute absolute speed difference for every valid vehicle record
SELECT
  state_number,
  consecutive_number,
  vehicle_number,
  ABS(travel_speed - speed_limit) AS abs_speed_diff
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.vehicle_2016`
WHERE travel_speed BETWEEN 0 AND 151
  AND speed_limit BETWEEN 5 AND 80
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Description: average the absolute speed difference per accident and bucket it
WITH veh AS (
  SELECT
    state_number,
    consecutive_number,
    AVG(ABS(travel_speed - speed_limit)) AS avg_abs_speed_diff
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.vehicle_2016`
  WHERE travel_speed BETWEEN 0 AND 151
    AND speed_limit BETWEEN 5 AND 80
  GROUP BY state_number, consecutive_number
)
SELECT
  state_number,
  consecutive_number,
  avg_abs_speed_diff,
  CASE
    WHEN avg_abs_speed_diff < 20 THEN 0
    WHEN avg_abs_speed_diff < 40 THEN 1
    WHEN avg_abs_speed_diff < 60 THEN 2
    WHEN avg_abs_speed_diff < 80 THEN 3
    ELSE 4
  END AS speed_diff_bucket
FROM veh
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Description: derive a binary work‑zone flag in the accident table
SELECT
  state_number,
  consecutive_number,
  work_zone,
  CASE WHEN work_zone = 0 THEN 0 ELSE 1 END AS work_zone_flag
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2016`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Description: combine person counts, fatality counts, and label for crashes with >1 person
WITH person_stats AS (
  SELECT
    state_number,
    consecutive_number,
    COUNT(DISTINCT person_number) AS persons_in_crash,
    COUNTIF(injury_severity = 4)   AS fatalities_in_crash
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.person_2016`
  GROUP BY state_number, consecutive_number
)
SELECT
  state_number,
  consecutive_number,
  persons_in_crash,
  fatalities_in_crash,
  CASE WHEN fatalities_in_crash > 1 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS y_label
FROM person_stats
WHERE persons_in_crash > 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Description: FINAL – build the labeled feature set for modelling
WITH person_stats AS (
  SELECT
    state_number,
    consecutive_number,
    COUNT(DISTINCT person_number) AS persons_in_crash,
    COUNTIF(injury_severity = 4)   AS fatalities_in_crash
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.person_2016`
  GROUP BY state_number, consecutive_number
  HAVING persons_in_crash > 1
),
speed_feat AS (
  SELECT
    state_number,
    consecutive_number,
    CASE
      WHEN AVG(ABS(travel_speed - speed_limit)) < 20 THEN 0
      WHEN AVG(ABS(travel_speed - speed_limit)) < 40 THEN 1
      WHEN AVG(ABS(travel_speed - speed_limit)) < 60 THEN 2
      WHEN AVG(ABS(travel_speed - speed_limit)) < 80 THEN 3
      ELSE 4
    END AS speed_diff_bucket
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.vehicle_2016`
  WHERE travel_speed BETWEEN 0 AND 151
    AND speed_limit BETWEEN 5 AND 80
  GROUP BY state_number, consecutive_number
),
body_type_pick AS (
  -- choose the first vehicle’s body_type per crash (one simple strategy)
  SELECT
    state_number,
    consecutive_number,
    ANY_VALUE(body_type) AS body_type
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.vehicle_2016`
  GROUP BY state_number, consecutive_number
)
SELECT
  a.state_number,
  b.body_type,
  a.number_of_drunk_drivers,
  a.day_of_week,
  a.hour_of_crash,
  CASE WHEN a.work_zone = 0 THEN 0 ELSE 1 END AS work_zone_flag,
  sf.speed_diff_bucket,
  ps.y_label
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2016` AS a
JOIN person_stats                             AS ps USING (state_number, consecutive_number)
LEFT JOIN speed_feat                          AS sf USING (state_number, consecutive_number)
LEFT JOIN body_type_pick                      AS b  USING (state_number, consecutive_number)
LIMIT 20;
```

The last query returns one row per accident, limited to 20 rows for inspection, with all requested predictors and the binary label (y_label = 1 if the crash involved more than one fatality and more than one person).
